This is the README file for the Dials distribution.

Dials shows how the resources of a GNU/Linux system are being used, as
they change.  It runs full screen in a terminal: a menu bar at the
top, the function keys at the bottom, and between them one page for
each part of the system:

  Overview    host, uptime, load; processor, memory and swap use
  CPU         use per processor core, frequency, temperature
  Memory      how memory is used, swap, memory pressure
  GPU         graphics processors and the processes using them
  Disks       throughput and latency per device, file system space
  Network     traffic, errors and addresses per interface
  Processes   a table of processes that can be sorted and filtered
  Sensors     temperatures, fan speeds and voltages
  Power       battery state and power use

This is an early version: so far the Overview and GPU pages show
measurements.  The GPU page shows NVIDIA graphics processors, using
the monitoring library that comes with the NVIDIA driver when it is
installed.

Dials reads its measurements from /proc and /sys.  It needs a C
compiler, pkg-config and the wide-character curses library (ncursesw)
to build.

  See the file ./INSTALL for building and installation instructions,
  and the file ./README-hacking for building from the source repository.

After installation, 'info dials' shows the manual and 'dials --help'
summarizes the command-line options.  Press F1 while Dials runs for
the list of keys.

Dials is free software.  See the file COPYING for copying conditions.
The manual may be copied under the terms of the GNU Free Documentation
License; see the manual itself.

